    {
      "sample_id": "MAT214-26601fa066554b7e",
      "question": "The source distinguishes between two uses of the term “linear.” How does it contrast a “linear function” (whose graph is a line) with a “linear transformation” from R into R, and how does Theorem 4.2 demonstrate that functions defined by matrices fit the latter category?",
      "reference_answer": "The source notes that a “linear function” may simply be one whose graph is a line, but such a function is not a linear transformation from R into R if it fails to preserve vector addition and scalar multiplication. By contrast, the source states that a linear transformation is operation preserving. Theorem 4.2 establishes that any function defined by an m×n matrix is a linear transformation from Rn into Rm; therefore matrix-defined functions automatically satisfy the operation-preserving requirements and fall into the category of linear transformations rather than merely “linear” functions in the graph sense.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source_id": "fde11f1a-2cf2-44aa-a948-24887e6143ad",
          "chunk_id": "0627699c-1967-482d-8fc4-5ae5d5f7ac02",
          "quote": "(1) is called a linear function because its graph is a line. But\n(2) is not a linear transformation from a vector space R into R because it preserves neither vector addition nor scalar multiplication.",
          "locator": "slide 1",
          "evidence_group": null
        },
        {
          "source_id": "fde11f1a-2cf2-44aa-a948-24887e6143ad",
          "chunk_id": "0627699c-1967-482d-8fc4-5ae5d5f7ac02",
          "quote": "(1) A linear transformation is said to be operation preserving.",
          "locator": "slide 1",
          "evidence_group": null
